Chapter: DeskWindowDragTest
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Dragging a window by its titlebar. The gesture is a state machine over two
 `ds` cells plus arithmetic over the content box, so where a window lands for
 a given pointer is decidable here without a screen, a face or a pointer.

 IT CALLS `dk-drag-to-x` AND `dk-drag-to-y`, WHICH ARE THE FUNCTIONS
 `desk-wnd-drag-step` ITSELF CALLS. The step is effectful and wants a screen,
 so the arithmetic was named rather than left inline: a test that re-derived
 `mx - dk-drag-dx ds` beside it would be a SHADOW of the painter's arithmetic,
 free to drift from it while both stayed green, which is how a golden comes to
 pin whatever the machine says today.

 The rows below print the PAYLOAD -- the resulting origin, and the offset the
 gesture is supposed to preserve -- rather than a verdict computed from it. A
 row printing only "the window moved" would agree with an implementation that
 dropped the grab offset and put the window's corner under the pointer, which
 is the one wrong answer a drag can give that still looks like a drag.

Section: Body

  dg-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dg-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

  dg-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-rect (blk) (w) (h) (ds) =
    let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in "x " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rx blk w st ds)
       & " y " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds)
       & " w " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rw blk w st ds)
       & " h " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds)

 THE OFFSET ROUND-TRIPS, and the pair is chosen so a packer that answered one
 field for both cannot pass: 37 and 9 disagree. The second pair asks the width
 of the low field, because `dx` is bounded by the window's width and a window
 on a wide screen is wider than a byte.

  dg-off : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-off (ds) (dx) (dy) =
    let g = dk-drag-begin ds desk-focus-clkp dx dy
    in "begin " & integer-to-text dx & "," & integer-to-text dy
       & " -> dx " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-dx ds)
       & " dy " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-dy ds)
       & "  exact " & dg-yn (dk-drag-dx ds == dx & dk-drag-dy ds == dy)
       & "  dragging " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-fid ds)

 THE GRAB POINT IS INVARIANT, AND THAT IS WHAT "DRAG" MEANS. The window's
 origin may be anything; what may not change is the distance from that origin
 to the point the person is holding. Ten samples, and the row prints how many
 of the ten held it.

 A count of ten proves nothing on its own, because a clamp that pinned every
 sample to one corner would also report a constant offset if the offset were
 recomputed from the pinned rect. So the row prints the FIRST and LAST origins
 too: they must differ, and the clamp rows below are the arm where this same
 invariant is supposed to BREAK.

  dg-inv : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dg-inv (ds) (w) (h) (ww) (wh) (dx) (dy) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 10 then acc
    else
      let mx = 300 + i * 57
      in let my = 200 + i * 23
      in let nx = dk-drag-to-x ds mx ww w
      in let ny = dk-drag-to-y ds my wh w h
      in dg-inv ds w h ww wh dx dy (i + 1) (if mx - nx == dx & my - ny == dy then acc + 1 else acc)

  dg-follow : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-follow (ds) (w) (h) (ww) (wh) (dx) (dy) =
    let g = dk-drag-begin ds desk-focus-clkp dx dy
    in let x0 = dk-drag-to-x ds 300 ww w
    in let y0 = dk-drag-to-y ds 200 wh w h
    in let x9 = dk-drag-to-x ds (300 + 9 * 57) ww w
    in let y9 = dk-drag-to-y ds (200 + 9 * 23) wh w h
    in "offset held " & integer-to-text (dg-inv ds w h ww wh dx dy 0 0) & " of 10"
       & "  first " & integer-to-text x0 & "," & integer-to-text y0
       & "  last " & integer-to-text x9 & "," & integer-to-text y9
       & "  they differ " & dg-yn (x0 /= x9 & y0 /= y9)

 A STILL POINTER MOVES NOTHING, which is the condition the step repaints on.
 Without it a held button repaints the whole desk at the pump rate for a
 pointer that is not moving.

  dg-still : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-still (ds) (w) (h) (ww) (wh) =
    let a = dk-drag-to-x ds 500 ww w
    in let b = dk-drag-to-x ds 500 ww w
    in let c = dk-drag-to-y ds 400 wh w h
    in let d = dk-drag-to-y ds 400 wh w h
    in "same pointer twice: " & integer-to-text a & "," & integer-to-text c
       & " then " & integer-to-text b & "," & integer-to-text d
       & "  unchanged " & dg-yn (a == b & c == d)

 THE CLAMP, asked at all four edges with a control in the middle. Each operand
 is far enough outside that the clamped and unclamped answers disagree by
 hundreds of pixels rather than by one, and the row prints the bound it is
 supposed to have hit so the number is checkable rather than merely stable.

 THE CONTROL IS THE ROW THAT MATTERS. A clamp that pinned everything would
 pass all four edges; the middle sample is what says it discriminates.

  dg-edge : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-edge (ds) (w) (h) (ww) (wh) (label) (mx) (my) =
    label & ": pointer " & integer-to-text mx & "," & integer-to-text my
       & " -> " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-to-x ds mx ww w)
       & "," & integer-to-text (dk-drag-to-y ds my wh w h)

  dg-bounds : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-bounds (w) (h) (ww) (wh) (ds) =
    "box x " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-x w ds)
    & " y " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-y w ds)
    & " w " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-w w ds)
    & " h " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-h w h ds)
    & "  x in " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-keep - ww)
    & ".." & integer-to-text (w - dk-drag-keep)
    & "  y in " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-y w ds)
    & ".." & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-y w ds + dk-cbox-h w h ds - wh)
    & "  keep " & integer-to-text dk-drag-keep

 IT CANNOT HIDE, WHICH IS THE REQUIREMENT AND NOT THE FOUR NUMBERS ABOVE.
 A window may hang off the glass now, so the thing worth asserting is not
 where the bounds happen to sit but that whatever the pointer does, some of
 the window is still on the screen to grab. This measures the ON-SCREEN
 rectangle at each extreme and asks for at least `dk-drag-keep` of it on both
 axes.

 It is an invariant against the CONSTANT rather than against a recorded
 coordinate, so it cannot rot the way `far right -> 1216` can.

  dg-vis : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dg-vis (a) (len) (limit) =
    let lo = if a < 0 then 0 else a
    in let hi = if a + len > limit then limit else a + len
    in if hi < lo then 0 else hi - lo

  dg-hide : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-hide (ds) (w) (h) (ww) (wh) =
    let lw = dg-vis (dk-drag-to-x ds 0 ww w) ww w
    in let rw = dg-vis (dk-drag-to-x ds 3000 ww w) ww w
    in let uh = dg-vis (dk-drag-to-y ds 0 wh w h) wh h
    in let dh = dg-vis (dk-drag-to-y ds 3000 wh w h) wh h
    in "cannot hide: left " & integer-to-text lw
       & " right " & integer-to-text rw
       & " up " & integer-to-text uh
       & " down " & integer-to-text dh
       & "  all at least " & integer-to-text dk-drag-keep & " "
       & dg-yn (lw >= dk-drag-keep & rw >= dk-drag-keep & uh >= dk-drag-keep & dh >= dk-drag-keep)

 A MAXIMISED WINDOW IS NOT DRAGGED, and the refusal in `desk-wnd-chrome-step`
 is a guard this row cannot reach: the step is effectful. What it CAN ask is
 the reason the guard exists, which is not circular. `dk-wnd-rx` reads the
 content box on the maximised arm whatever the block holds, so a rectangle
 written by a drag would be a rectangle nothing reads. The row writes one and
 asks what the window's rect answers.

  dg-max : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-max (w) (h) (ds) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    in let p = dk-wnd-place blk 10 10 400 300
    in let m = poke-32 blk dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-max
    in "placed at 10,10 while maximised: " & dg-rect blk w h ds
       & "  the block still says " & integer-to-text (peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rx-cell)
       & "," & integer-to-text (peek-32 blk dk-wnd-ry-cell)
       & "  the rect ignores it " & dg-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-max ds == dk-cbox-x w ds)

 A NEGATIVE X SURVIVES THE CELL, which is the whole of the crash this arm
 exists for. `peek-32` zero-extends, so a rect stored at -400 read back as
 4294966896 and every consumer of it was wrong in a different way: the address
 arithmetic faulted the guest, the clip silently drew nothing, and the window
 disappeared. None of the rows above could see it, because none of them stores
 a negative and reads it BACK -- they compute a position and print it.

 The row asks for the round trip and for the sign, not for the number alone: a
 reader that dropped the sign would still print a plausible coordinate.

  dg-neg : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dg-neg (w) (h) (ds) =
    let blk = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    in let p = dk-wnd-place blk (0 - 400) 148 1200 500
    in let rx = dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-normal ds
    in let ry = dk-wnd-ry blk w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds
    in "placed at -400,148 reads back " & integer-to-text rx & "," & integer-to-text ry
       & "  raw cell " & integer-to-text (peek-32 blk dk-wnd-rx-cell)
       & "  negative " & dg-yn (rx < 0)
       & "  exact " & dg-yn (rx == 0 - 400 & ry == 148)

  dg-release : Integer -> Text
  dg-release (ds) =
    let e = dk-drag-end ds
    in "released: dragging " & integer-to-text (dk-drag-fid ds)
       & "  none " & integer-to-text desk-focus-none
       & "  ended " & dg-yn (dk-drag-fid ds == desk-focus-none)

Section: Opening

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let a1 = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let a2 = poke-32 ds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let a3 = poke-32 ds 24 (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let w = 1280
    in let h = 800
    in let ww = 400
    in let wh = 300
    in act
      print-line-uni (dg-bounds w h ww wh ds)
      print-line-uni (dg-off ds 37 9)
      print-line-uni (dg-off ds 1200 3)
      print-line-uni (dg-follow ds w h ww wh 37 9)
      print-line-uni (dg-still ds w h ww wh)
      print-line-uni (dg-edge ds w h ww wh "far left  " 0 400)
      print-line-uni (dg-edge ds w h ww wh "far right " 3000 400)
      print-line-uni (dg-edge ds w h ww wh "far up    " 500 0)
      print-line-uni (dg-edge ds w h ww wh "far down  " 500 3000)
      print-line-uni (dg-edge ds w h ww wh "the middle" 500 400)
      print-line-uni (dg-hide ds w h ww wh)
      let nds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
      in let n1 = poke-32 nds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
      in print-line-uni (dg-neg w h nds)
      print-line-uni (dg-release ds)
      let mds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
      in let b1 = poke-32 mds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
      in print-line-uni (dg-max w h mds)
    end

